// G9'S PICTURE PROOF (step 5, 2026-09-28): the HDR encode with the per-pixel restoration skip against the encode as it was,
// on the GPU, pixel for pixel, with no game. The savings research's critic said the offline interpreter cannot see the GPU's
// approximate rcp and that "a GPU screenshot pair is the only real picture proof"; this is that pair without the owner.
//
// The skip is the committed headers kHdrEncodePS / kHdrEncodeCgaPS. The encode before it, kHdrEncodeNoSkipPS /
// kHdrEncodeCgaNoSkipPS, is generated by build.ps1 from today's source with the skip's condition put back (Stage F,
// 2026-09-30: B3 switch 11's committed control headers left the source; at step5-instruments the generated pair was
// byte-identical to them, and the scratchpad's g9_hdrproof.py had proved those equal to the encodes committed before the
// skip on 2026-09-28, before the Xbox mask fix and the restoration switch changed the encode; this program does not re-check
// that). Drawn as the mod draws them: the shared full-screen vertex shader
// (kIvtFsVS) with its FLOAT4 declaration and quad, UV from VPOS, the nine constant rows HdrFillConstants fills, every sampler
// the encode declares bound; all sampled POINT and CLAMP (the mod filters some linearly), which is the same for both encodes. Into an FP32 target (every bit the shader computes) and an FP16 target (the picture
// the swapchain gets), then read back.
//
// THE FRAME holds the cases that decide the skip: gradients below white; saturated emitters above white (a tail light,
// one channel only); noise across white; 8x4 blocks (one NVIDIA warp) that are sub-white but for ONE pixel, which makes the
// whole warp take the restoration while its neighbours skip; exactly 1.0 and the next FP16 step above it; the knee region
// of source 1 (a brightest channel decoding to 0.85..0.95 around kHdrKnee 0.90); zero, tiny negatives, 65504, +Inf, NaN.
// THE STATES are every path the skip touches: source 0 (the owner's), sRGB and 2.2 and 2.4 decodes, roll-off on and off,
// restoration 1 and 0.5 and off, the HUD mask on, a game menu, source 1's exact ratio (the knee), source 1's LUT path (which
// never skips), each console gamma mode, and the absorbed encode (FusionFix's curve at its one sample) with dither on.
//
// THE PROOF: in every state, the skip's FP32 output equals the old one within 4 float32 steps (the GPU's rcp can make x/x
// read one step above 1, which the skip then removes), NaN where the old one had NaN, and the FP16 picture differs only by
// one FP16 step, only as often as those float32 steps can cross an FP16 rounding edge (the bound is derived in main).
// Through DXVK only: native D3D9 refuses this harness's draw (INVALIDCALL, not chased), and the mod runs on DXVK alone. TWO CONTROLS prove the comparison can see a difference: the old
// encode at restoration 1.0 against 0.99 must differ above white, and the frame must hold warps of both kinds.
//
//     tools\test-hdr-skip\build.ps1           builds g9.exe (x86, as the game)
//     g9.exe --d3d9 <path> [--timing]         <path>: DXVK's d3d9, the game folder's vulkan.dll (the game's own translation);
//                                             without it native D3D9 is tried, which refuses the draw on this machine;
//                                             --timing adds a 4K timing (noise at full clock: the in-game A/B prices it)
// Exit 0 when every proof passed. It changes nothing on the machine.
